<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Batman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1801">1801</a>–<a href="/years/1839">1839</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Batman was an Australian grazier and explorer born in New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-wales">South Wales</a> who settled in Van Diemen's Land. In 1835 he crossed Bass Strait and signed a 'treaty' with Wurundjeri elders to acquire some 240,000 hectares around Port Phillip, which the colonial government declared void. His party helped found the settlement that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-batman.jpg" alt="John Batman" width="320" height="387" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Nuttall · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Batman_portrait.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Batman was born on 21 January 1801 in Parramatta, the son of William and Mary Batman. His father had been sentenced to fourteen years' transportation for receiving stolen saltpetre and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a> aboard the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> on 2 June 1797; he later ran a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>-yard at Parramatta and became a devout Wesleyan. Aboriginal people came into Parramatta for charity and education, and the young Batman knew many of them. In 1816 he was apprenticed as a blacksmith in Sydney.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In December 1821 Batman and his younger brother Henry went to Van Diemen's Land to settle as graziers near Ben Lomond in the north-east, where he acquired a property called Kingston. In early 1826 he captured the bushranger Matthew Brady, who was later hanged at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hobart">Hobart</a>, and was rewarded with a further grant of land. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-war">Black War</a> he led roving parties, with Aboriginal men hired in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a>, that hunted Tasmanian Aboriginal people, and in 1830 he took part in the Black Line.</p><p>In September 1829 he led a night attack on a family group of 60 to 70 men, women and children in the Ben Lomond district. By his own report 15 were killed, and he shot two wounded men he had captured when they could not walk. A captured woman, Luggenemenener, was gaoled and separated from her two-year-old son Rolepana, whom Batman kept. Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-arthur">George Arthur</a> observed that Batman &quot;had much slaughter to account for&quot;, and his neighbour, the artist John Glover, called him a murderer of blacks.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After the New South Wales authorities refused him land at Western Port, Batman sailed for the mainland in 1835 in the schooner Rebecca as a leading member of the Port Phillip Association. In June 1835, on the banks of the Merri Creek, he negotiated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulin">Kulin</a> elders for a yearly rent of blankets, axes, knives, mirrors, flour and other goods. It is unlikely that the Kulin understood this as a transfer of land, and Governor Bourke held the treaty invalid because the land was claimed by the Crown.</p><p>Batman settled with his wife, the former convict Elizabeth Callaghan, and their seven daughters at what became Batman's Hill. Syphilis, diagnosed in 1833, crippled and disfigured him, and he died on 6 May 1839. In the 21st century his killings in Tasmania have been the focus of criticism: in 2016 Batman Park in Northcote was renamed Gumbri Park, and in 2018 the electoral Division of Batman became the Division of Cooper.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Batman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Settlers from Van Diemen's Land, led by <b>John Batman</b> and John Pascoe Fawkner, founded Melbourne in 1835 on Kulin land, and the area was governed as the Port Phillip District of the Colony of New South Wales.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-victoria">Colony of Victoria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Parramatta, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melbourne">Melbourne</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/australia">Australia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-33.82,151&amp;year=1825" aria-label="Parramatta on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John Batman"><image href="/maps/globes/g150s30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5071 5326h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5071 5326h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-33.82,151&amp;year=1825">What was here in 1825? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
